A module of console output, decimal formatting, and byte-safe input.

Use core.

To format_u64_into given n as an unsigned number, buf as a pointer to byte, returning an unsigned number.
    Define local digits as block of 20 byte.
    Define local count as an unsigned number.
    Define local value as an unsigned number.
    Set value to n.
    If value == 0:
        Set item 0 of buf to 48.
        Give back 1.
    While value > 0:
        Set item count of digits to 48 + (value % 10) as a byte.
        Set value to value / 10.
        Add 1 to count.
    Define local len as an unsigned number.
    While count > 0:
        Set count to count - 1.
        Set item len of buf to item count of digits.
        Add 1 to len.
    Give back len.
Done.

Publicly, to format_i64_into given n as a whole number, buf as a pointer to byte, returning an unsigned number.
    Define local len as an unsigned number.
    Define local magnitude as an unsigned number.
    If n < 0:
        Set item 0 of buf to 45.
        Set len to 1 as an unsigned number.
        Set magnitude to (0 as an unsigned number) - (n as an unsigned number).
    Else:
        Set magnitude to n as an unsigned number.
    Define local written as an unsigned number.
    Set written to format_u64_into(magnitude, (buf as an unsigned number + len) as a pointer to byte).
    Give back len + written.
Done.

Publicly, to format_f64_into given x as a decimal, buf as a pointer to byte, returning an unsigned number.
    Define local negative as a truth value.
    Define local absolute as a decimal.
    If x < 0.0:
        Set negative to true.
        Set absolute to 0.0 - x.
    Else:
        Set absolute to x.
    Define local scaled as an unsigned number.
    Set scaled to (absolute * 10000.0 + 0.5) as an unsigned number.
    Define local int_part as an unsigned number.
    Set int_part to scaled / 10000.
    Define local frac as an unsigned number.
    Set frac to scaled % 10000.
    Define local len as an unsigned number.
    If negative and scaled != 0:
        Set item 0 of buf to 45.
        Set len to 1 as an unsigned number.
    Define local text_len as an unsigned number.
    Set text_len to format_u64_into(int_part, (buf as an unsigned number + len) as a pointer to byte).
    Set len to len + text_len.
    Set item len of buf to 46.
    Set len to len + 1.
    Define local divisor as an unsigned number.
    Set divisor to 1000.
    While divisor > 0:
        Set item len of buf to (48 + frac / divisor) as a byte.
        Set frac to frac % divisor.
        Set divisor to divisor / 10.
        Add 1 to len.
    Give back len.
Done.

To newline returning nothing.
    Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "\n", and 1.
Done.

Publicly, to print_i64 given n as a whole number, returning nothing.
    You can call this as "say <n>", "show <n>", "print <n>", or "display <n>".
    Define local buf as block of 32 byte.
    Define local len as an unsigned number.
    Set len to format_i64_into(n, address of item 0 of buf).
    Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.
    newline().
Done.

Publicly, to print_u64 given n as an unsigned number, returning nothing.
    You can call this as "say <n>", "show <n>", "print <n>", or "display <n>".
    Define local buf as block of 32 byte.
    Define local len as an unsigned number.
    Set len to format_u64_into(n, address of item 0 of buf).
    Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.
    newline().
Done.

Publicly, to print_f64 given x as a decimal, returning nothing.
    You can call this as "say <x>", "show <x>", "print <x>", or "display <x>".
    Define local buf as block of 64 byte.
    Define local len as an unsigned number.
    Set len to format_f64_into(x, address of item 0 of buf).
    Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, address of item 0 of buf, and len.
    newline().
Done.

Publicly, to print_bool given b as a truth value, returning nothing.
    You can call this as "say <b>", "show <b>", "print <b>", or "display <b>".
    If b:
        Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "true", and 4.
    Else:
        Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, "false", and 5.
    newline().
Done.

Publicly, to print_bytes given p as a pointer to byte, len as an unsigned number, returning nothing.
    Call this as "say bytes <p> of length <len>".
    Ask Linux kernel 1 with 1, p, and len.
    newline().
Done.

Publicly, to read_i64_stdin returning a whole number.
    Define local ch as block of 1 byte.
    Define local value as a whole number.
    Define local sign as a whole number.
    Define local seen as a truth value.
    Set sign to 1.
    Loop:
        Define local read_count as a whole number.
        Set read_count to ask Linux kernel 0 with 0, address of item 0 of ch, and 1.
        If read_count <= 0:
            break.
        Define local code as a whole number.
        Set code to (item 0 of ch) as a whole number.
        If not seen:
            If code == 45:
                Set sign to -1.
                continue.
            If code == 43:
                continue.
            If code == 32 or code == 9 or code == 13 or code == 10:
                continue.
            Set seen to true.
        If code >= 48 and code <= 57:
            Set value to value * 10 + (code - 48).
            continue.
        break.
    Give back value * sign.
Done.

Publicly, to read_f64_stdin returning a decimal.
    Define local ch as block of 1 byte.
    Define local value as a decimal.
    Define local sign as a decimal.
    Define local divisor as a decimal.
    Define local seen as a truth value.
    Define local fractional as a truth value.
    Set sign to 1.0.
    Set divisor to 10.0.
    Loop:
        Define local read_count as a whole number.
        Set read_count to ask Linux kernel 0 with 0, address of item 0 of ch, and 1.
        If read_count <= 0:
            break.
        Define local code as a whole number.
        Set code to (item 0 of ch) as a whole number.
        If not seen and not fractional:
            If code == 45:
                Set sign to -1.0.
                continue.
            If code == 43:
                continue.
            If code == 32 or code == 9 or code == 13 or code == 10:
                continue.
            If code == 46:
                Set seen to true.
                Set fractional to true.
                continue.
            Set seen to true.
        If code >= 48 and code <= 57:
            If fractional:
                Set value to value + (code - 48) as a decimal / divisor.
                Set divisor to divisor * 10.0.
            Else:
                Set value to value * 10.0 + (code - 48) as a decimal.
            continue.
        If code == 46 and not fractional:
            Set fractional to true.
            continue.
        break.
    Give back value * sign.
Done.

Publicly, to read_i64_into given dst as a pointer to whole number, returning nothing.
    Call this as "read number into <dst>".
    Call this as "ask for <dst>".
    Set item 0 of dst to read_i64_stdin().
Done.
